# Docker Interview Questions and Answers – .NET 8 + AWS Project

I'll explain Docker interview questions in a real-time project scenario and natural spoken-English style, so you can answer confidently in technical interviews.

Since your project uses ASP.NET Core Web API (.NET 8), React.js / ASP.NET Core MVC, AWS EC2, ECR, RDS SQL Server, and Jenkins, I'll connect the Docker concepts to this architecture.

## 1. Docker fundamentals

### Q1. What is Docker?

Spoken answer:

Docker is a containerization platform that allows us to package an application along with its dependencies, libraries, configuration, and runtime into a single unit called a container image.

In my project, we developed an ASP.NET Core Web API using .NET 8. Instead of deploying the application directly onto the EC2 server, we created a Docker image.

We run that image as a container on EC2.

The main advantage is that the application behaves consistently across development, testing, staging, and production environments.

### Q2. What is a Docker container?

A Docker container is a running instance of a Docker image.

For example, we create a Docker image for our .NET 8 Web API. When we run that image, Docker creates a container.

Inside that container, our ASP.NET Core application runs with its required runtime and dependencies.

We can run multiple containers using the same image.

### Q3. What is the difference between Docker image and container?

| Docker image | Docker container |
| --- | --- |
| It is a blueprint. | It is a running instance. |
| It is generally immutable. | It has a writable container layer. |
| Created using Dockerfile. | Created using an image. |
| Can be stored in ECR. | Runs on EC2 or another container host. |

Spoken answer:

I would say a Docker image is like a template, and a Docker container is the actual running application created from that template.

For example, we build one .NET API image and use that same image to run multiple containers.

### Q4. What is a Dockerfile?

A Dockerfile is a text file containing instructions to build a Docker image.

For example, in our .NET 8 project, we use a Dockerfile to define:

- Base .NET SDK image.
- Working directory.
- Application source code.
- NuGet package restoration.
- Application build and publish.
- Runtime image.
- Application startup command.

Example:

dockerfile

```dockerfile
FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:8.0 AS build

WORKDIR /src

COPY . .

RUN dotnet restore

RUN dotnet publish -c Release -o /app/publish

FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0

WORKDIR /app

COPY --from=build /app/publish .

EXPOSE 8080

ENTRYPOINT ["dotnet", "MyApi.dll"]
```

### Q5. What is the difference between Docker and a virtual machine?

Setup ROS2 in a Docker Container - Devansh Dhrafani

[devanshdhrafani.com](<https://devanshdhrafani.com/posts/2021-04-15-dockerros2/?utm_source=chatgpt.com>)

Spoken answer:

The main difference is that a virtual machine has its own guest operating system, whereas Docker containers share the host operating system kernel.

Virtual machines are generally heavier and require more resources.

Docker containers are lightweight, start faster, and make application deployment easier.

In our project, we used Docker containers to run our .NET applications on AWS EC2.

### Q6. What are the main advantages of Docker?

Spoken answer:

The main advantages of Docker are portability, lightweight deployment, scalability, isolation, consistency, and faster application delivery.

In our project, Docker helped us package our frontend and backend applications separately and deploy them consistently across different environments.

